# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hoverlab environment
#
# Copy to .env and fill in. Everything except the Firebase keys is optional —
# the app runs with analytics and billing switched off when their keys are
# absent, rather than erroring.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Required: Firebase -----------------------------------------------------
#
# Accounts, sign-in and password reset are Firebase Auth; favourites, bundles
# and billing records live in Firestore.
#
# Note where the work happens: the SERVER calls Firebase, not the browser.
# The Firebase client SDK talks to identitytoolkit.googleapis.com directly,
# which makes sign-in fail for anyone running an ad blocker, a privacy
# extension or filtered DNS — a failure the app cannot detect or fix. Sign-in
# is server-side for that reason, so only these two values are needed.
#
# Setup (console.firebase.google.com):
#   1. Create a project.
#   2. Authentication → Sign-in method → enable Email/Password.
#   3. Firestore Database → create a database.
#   4. Project settings → General → Web API key → the value below.
#   5. Project settings → Service accounts → Generate new private key. That
#      JSON file IS a secret — it bypasses all security rules.
#
# Passkeys need no extra configuration — they are verified by this server
# against public keys in Firestore, not by Firebase, which has no notion of
# WebAuthn. Two collections appear on their own: `passkeys` (the registered
# credentials) and `webauthnChallenges` (in-flight sign-in attempts). The
# second is worth a TTL policy on `expiresAt` — Firestore Database → TTL —
# purely so abandoned attempts do not accumulate. Nothing depends on it:
# expiry is enforced when a challenge is read.

# Public by design: it identifies the project and is useless without
# credentials. NEXT_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY is read as a fallback.
FIREBASE_API_KEY=""

# Server credentials. Use ONE of the following.
#
# Local development — path to the downloaded key file. Keep it out of git;
# .gitignore already covers *service-account*.json.
FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_FILE="./firebase-service-account.json"
#
# Hosting (Vercel) — the same JSON as a single-line string, since there is no
# file to point at. Paste the whole file, starting with {"type":"service_account".
# FIREBASE_SERVICE_ACCOUNT=""


# --- Canonical URL ----------------------------------------------------------
# The origin that canonical tags, OG image URLs, sitemap.xml entries, and the
# post-checkout return URL are all built from. No trailing slash.
#
# ON VERCEL, LEAVE THIS UNSET unless you are serving a custom domain. Without
# it, src/lib/site.ts falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL — the
# project's own production domain, which is always correct and never goes
# stale. Setting it by hand pins a domain that outlives its DNS: point it at a
# domain you have not attached yet and the sitemap advertises URLs that do not
# resolve, while checkout redirects a customer who has just paid to a dead
# page. Set it only once the domain actually resolves.
#
# Required everywhere else — off Vercel there is nothing to fall back to but
# http://localhost:3000.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL="https://hoverlab.dev"


# --- Operator (who the customer is contracting with) -------------------------
#
# SET THESE BEFORE TAKING REAL MONEY. They are the only launch requirement
# that no amount of code can satisfy.
#
# Terms, Privacy, Refunds and the Licence each name the legal person behind
# Hoverlab. Unset, they fall back to a detectable "TO BE SET" placeholder —
# so the pages still render and still say something true about their own
# incompleteness, rather than showing a blank where a company name goes.
#
# A payment processor reads these on merchant review, and a customer with a
# complaint has nowhere to send it without them. `npm run check:deploy`
# FAILS on the placeholder; `npm run check:env` warns.
#
# Server-side only, deliberately. They are read at module scope, so on a
# statically rendered route they are baked at build time: set them in the
# hosting dashboard and redeploy, don't expect a running site to pick them up.
#
# A registered company, or your own legal name if a sole trader.
OPERATOR_LEGAL_NAME=""
# One line. Most processors require a real postal address, not a PO box.
OPERATOR_ADDRESS=""
# Whose law governs and whose courts hear a dispute, e.g. "India".
OPERATOR_JURISDICTION=""
# Where a human replies: support, privacy requests, legal notices.
OPERATOR_CONTACT_EMAIL=""


# --- AI (Anthropic) ---------------------------------------------------------
# What Pro+ actually sells. Three routes call a model: /api/ai/variant
# (vary or recolour an effect, 1 credit), /api/ai/compose (a section from a
# brief, 3 credits) and /api/ai/search (semantic ranking on /library,
# metered rather than charged).
#
# Unset, all three return 503 BEFORE metering — no credit is spent, no
# quota is consumed, and /library falls back to substring search. That is a
# deliberate refusal rather than a caught failure: the previous SDK failed
# after charging and refunding, which is how Pro+ went on selling a feature
# whose every request failed politely.
#
# So: if you sell Pro+, set this. If you do not set it, do not sell Pro+
# (leave POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_PLUS empty and the tier stops rendering).
#
# Get a key at console.anthropic.com and set a spend limit on it while you
# are there — these routes are metered per user, not per dollar.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=""


# --- Analytics (PostHog) ----------------------------------------------------
# Omit to disable analytics entirely; no network calls are made without a key.
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST="https://us.i.posthog.com"


# --- Billing (Polar) --------------------------------------------------------
# Polar is the merchant of record: it collects and remits VAT / sales tax, so
# no per-jurisdiction tax registration is needed.
#
# Setup:
#  1. Create an organization at https://polar.sh
#  2. Create two products:
#       "Hoverlab Pro"  — one-time payment
#       "Hoverlab Team" — recurring monthly, per-seat
#  3. Copy each product id below.
#  4. Add a webhook endpoint pointing at:
#       https://<your-domain>/api/billing/webhook
#     subscribed to: order.paid, order.refunded, subscription.active,
#     subscription.updated, subscription.canceled, subscription.revoked,
#     subscription.past_due, subscription.uncanceled
#  5. Copy the webhook signing secret below.
#
# Billing routes return 503 until POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN and POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET
# are both set, and the pricing page falls back to a waitlist CTA for any tier
# whose product id is missing.

POLAR_ACCESS_TOKEN=""
POLAR_WEBHOOK_SECRET=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_PRO=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_TEAM=""
# Team sold as a one-time twelve-month term instead of a subscription.
# Leave empty and the annual option simply does not render — the monthly
# plan is unaffected. Worth setting: recurring cross-border card charges
# are unreliable from Indian and Brazilian cards, which is most of the
# audience this plan is for.
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_TEAM_ANNUAL=""

# The rest of the ladder. Every one of these is read by plans.ts and
# credits.ts and none of them was listed here, which is why they are worth
# spelling out: the regional DISCOUNT ids below already cover Plus, Studio
# and the renewals, so a setup that follows this file to the letter could
# end up with discounts configured for products that do not exist. A tier
# whose product id is blank does not render at all.
#
#   Plus     $9/mo recurring     500 AI credits a month
#   Studio   $299 one-time       ten seats, one payment
#   Renewal  $32 / $120 one-time another year of updates for Pro / Studio
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_PLUS=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_STUDIO=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_RENEWAL=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_RENEWAL_STUDIO=""

# Consumable credit packs, bought outright rather than subscribed to. Both
# are one-time products; leave either blank and that pack is not offered.
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_CREDITS_500=""
POLAR_PRODUCT_ID_CREDITS_2000=""

# --- Regional (PPP) pricing -------------------------------------------------
# Polar has no native purchasing-power-parity pricing, so a region is a
# DISCOUNT off the USD list price rather than a second product.
#
# Do not create these by hand. `npm run polar:provision` creates all thirty-five
# and prints them in exactly the shape below — the amounts are computed as
# list minus band from src/lib/billing/plans.ts, and hand-entering them is
# how the two drift apart.
#
# They are FIXED amounts, not percentages: the prices in plans.ts are round
# numbers and a percentage lands just off them ($79 less 68% is $25.28, not
# $25), so the page would advertise a price the receipt doesn't match.
#
# A region is a purchasing-power BAND, not a country — see the comment on
# `Region` in plans.ts, and src/lib/billing/region.ts for which country is in
# which band:
#
#   IN      band A, charged in rupees      Pro $25 / ₹2,400
#   PPP_A   band A, charged in dollars     Pro $25   (PK, BD, NG, EG, VN, …)
#   PPP_B   band B, charged in dollars     Pro $39   (BR, MX, TR, ZA, AR, …)
#   PPP_C   band C, charged in dollars     Pro $55   (PL, RO, MY, CL, PT, …)
#
# The country comes from the edge geolocation header (x-vercel-ip-country) on
# the server, never from the client, so the displayed price cannot be talked
# into a cheaper charge.
#
# Leaving one blank is safe: plans.ts falls back to the list price for
# display, so a missing id can never advertise $25 and charge $79. Leaving
# one blank is also SILENT — the visitor simply sees list price — which is
# why `npm run polar:provision` prints the full set on every run rather than
# only what it created.

POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_PRO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_PLUS=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_STUDIO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_TEAM=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_RENEWAL=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_RENEWAL_STUDIO=""

# India's rupee pair. A fixed Polar discount belongs to the currency it is
# denominated in, so the dollar id above is rejected by a rupee checkout with
# "Discount does not exist" — without these an Indian buyer pays list price.
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_PRO_INR=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_PLUS_INR=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_STUDIO_INR=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_TEAM_INR=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_RENEWAL_INR=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_IN_RENEWAL_STUDIO_INR=""

POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_PRO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_PLUS=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_STUDIO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_TEAM=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_RENEWAL=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_A_RENEWAL_STUDIO=""

POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_PRO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_PLUS=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_STUDIO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_TEAM=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_RENEWAL=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_B_RENEWAL_STUDIO=""

POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_PRO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_PLUS=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_STUDIO=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_TEAM=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_RENEWAL=""
POLAR_DISCOUNT_ID_PPP_C_RENEWAL_STUDIO=""

# Pretend every request comes from this country, so a regional path can be
# exercised locally — no edge proxy runs in dev, so without this everything
# resolves to list price in USD. Takes a country code, not a band: "BR"
# resolves to ppp-b the same way a real header would. Ignored when
# NODE_ENV=production. Leave blank unless testing.
#   DEV_PRICING_REGION="IN"
DEV_PRICING_REGION=""

# 'sandbox' (default) or 'production'. Defaults to sandbox so a misconfigured
# deploy cannot accidentally take real money.
POLAR_SERVER="sandbox"


# --- Newsletter (Resend) ----------------------------------------------------
# Subscribers are stored in Firestore first and mirrored to Resend second, so
# the list survives without this and no signup fails when Resend is down.
#
# Nothing sends without it, though: the author sequence in
# src/lib/sequences.ts is written and reviewable but has no transport, so
# every address collected until this is set is a list nobody has mailed.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
RESEND_AUDIENCE_ID=""


# --- shadcn registry --------------------------------------------------------
# `npm run submit:registry` needs no token for the FIRST submission — one
# POST, no account. registry.directory issues a bearer token on acceptance,
# and that token is what lets a later run update the listing rather than
# create a second one.
#
# The submission is audited by fetching NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL/registry.json
# from the public internet, so the domain above has to resolve first.
REGISTRY_DIRECTORY_TOKEN=""


# --- Community links --------------------------------------------------------
# Where the footer and the community band point. Each falls back to the
# service's home page when unset, so an unset one is a dead-ish link rather
# than a broken build.
NEXT_PUBLIC_GITHUB_URL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_DISCORD_URL=""
NEXT_PUBLIC_TWITTER_URL=""


# --- Export quota -----------------------------------------------------------
# Salt for the hash that buckets anonymous export quota by IP. Optional: an
# unsalted hash still meters correctly, and the built-in fallback is not
# pretended to be a secret. Set it to stop the buckets being guessable.
QUOTA_IP_SALT=""
